#### EEG bad-channel detection

Bad EEG channels were identified using random sample consensus (RANSAC)
({{ ref('FischlerBolles1981', 'Fischler, M. A., & Bolles, R. C. (1981). Random sample consensus: A paradigm for model fitting with applications to image analysis and automated cartography. Communications of the ACM, 24(6), 381–395. doi:10.1145/358669.358692') }}),
as implemented in Autoreject
({{ ref('Jas2017', 'Jas, M., Engemann, D. A., Bekhti, Y., Raimondo, F., & Gramfort, A. (2017). Autoreject: Automated artifact rejection for MEG and EEG data. NeuroImage, 159, 417–429. doi:10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.06.030') }}).

The continuous EEG data were divided into {{ s.settings.ransac_epoch_duration }} s segments for RANSAC estimation. For each channel, predictions were generated from random subsets containing {{ (100 * s.settings.min_channels) | round(1) }}% of the available EEG channels across {{ s.settings.n_resample }} resampling iterations. Channels with a prediction correlation below {{ s.settings.min_corr }} for more than {{ (100 * s.settings.unbroken_time) | round(1) }}% of the segments were classified as bad.

Detected bad EEG channels were reconstructed using spherical-spline interpolation.